sssd-smartcard (0.2.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Never report PKINIT success on the strength of a ticket existing. Without
    the krb5-pkinit plugin, kinit silently ignores -X X509_user_identity,
    prompts for the account password and returns a perfectly valid TGT; the
    previous code called that a PASS. The verdict now requires KRB5_TRACE to
    show a pkinit preauth module returning 0/Success, and a ticket obtained by
    PA-ENC-TIMESTAMP is reported as a failure naming what happened.
  * Refuse to run kinit at all when pkinit.so is missing, instead of prompting
    for a password during what is advertised as a Smart Card test.
  * Report the reason PKINIT failed, read from KRB5_TRACE, rather than printing
    a list of possible causes over the captured answer.
  * Inspect the KDC's own certificate over LDAPS and name the pkinit_* options
    it forces on the client. Microsoft's older Domain Controller template
    carries serverAuth and a dNSName instead of the id-pkinit-KPKdc EKU and
    id-pkinit-san that RFC 4556 specifies; Windows accepts it, MIT does not,
    because pkinit_eku_checking defaults to kpKDC.
  * New checks: PKINIT plugin presence, pkinit_anchors, and whether krb5.conf
    includes SSSD's own snippet directory (three snippets were inert on a
    fully working joined host).
  * Stop defaulting the PKCS#11 module to an RHEL-only path that does not exist
    on Debian or Ubuntu.
  * The desktop installer no longer marks the SSH interface unmanaged in
    NetworkManager. That setting survives a reboot, and so does the desktop
    install's switch of netplan's renderer TO NetworkManager -- together,
    nothing configured the interface and the machine came back with no address
    at all. Observed on a real server. The guard was unnecessary as well: an
    interface rendered by systemd-networkd is not claimed by NetworkManager.
    It is removed, --no-protect-iface is accepted and ignored, and the script
    deletes the file where an earlier run left it -- before the role check, so
    a machine stranded by it is not turned away before being repaired.
  * The desktop installer installs the desktop and openssh-server only. SSSD
    and the Smart Card packages belong to 'sssd-smartcard-setup --only
    packages', where they are version checked; installing them in two places
    made this script's scope unclear. openssh-server is deliberate: a machine
    that has just gained a graphical login but lost remote access needs a
    console to fix. Verification now confirms the SSH server is running.
  * Running a script standalone without its lib/ directory no longer dies with
    "env_default_path: command not found" before printing anything; the
    environment file is simply unavailable.
  * New docs/COMMANDS.md: a reference for all five programs and every
    subcommand, with the reasoning behind the non-obvious flags. The README
    loses three overlapping how-to sections that DEPLOY.md and this now cover,
    and gains a documentation map.
  * Support two machines in one domain, which is how the second round of tests
    is set up: a desktop host for greeter logins and a server host driven under
    sudo. The environment file gains a role key, the desktop installer refuses
    to run on a machine declared role: server, and ad-prepare warns when the
    hostname it is about to take already resolves elsewhere -- two machines
    joining under one name means the second takes over the first's account in
    AD, and the first then fails Kerberos with a KVNO mismatch that looks
    unrelated to names.
  * New 'ca discover': works a PKI out from one certificate and fetches it.
    Follows AIA caIssuers upwards to a self-signed root, collects the CRL
    distribution points of the leaf and every CA, and sends a real OCSP request
    to find out whether a responder answers -- an advertised URL only means the
    CA put one in the certificate, and SSSD fails closed on OCSP, so enabling it
    against a silent responder stops every card login. The recommended
    revocation method follows what the PKI can actually enforce, and says
    whether OCSP is absent or merely unresponsive. --pki-url supplies a base URL
    for a certificate whose AIA is missing or LDAP-only; --install writes the
    trust bundle and the CRLs. Handles PEM, DER and PKCS#7, since AD CS serves
    all three. Verified against a live two-tier PKI: the fetched chain's
    fingerprints matched a bundle previously verified against AD's NTAuth store
    by a different route. The report states that downloaded is not trusted and
    prints fingerprints for out-of-band comparison, because anything in the
    bundle can issue a certificate that logs in.
  * The domain is described once in a JSON file rather than retyped on every
    command line: /etc/sssd-smartcard/environment.json, read by ad-prepare,
    ad-reset and the desktop installer. 'ad-prepare --init-env' writes a
    template. Unknown keys are refused rather than ignored, since a typo that
    is silently dropped leaves the operator believing a setting took effect;
    the realm is derived from the domain when absent; a flag on the command
    line still overrides the file. Parsing is done by a small python3 helper,
    not in shell.
  * New scripts/ad-reset.sh, installed as /usr/sbin/ad-reset: removes every
    trace of one AD domain so the machine can be reused for another. Reusing
    a machine without it leaves traces in thirteen places, and the ones that
    mislead are not the obvious ones -- the SSSD cache keeps answering for the
    old domain's users, the keytab holds the old host principal, krb5.conf
    still names the old realm and KDC, and the certmap rule still pins the old
    issuer DN, so a certificate from a new PKI silently fails to match and
    login falls through to a password. 'realm leave' runs first, while the old
    DC is still reachable, so the machine account is deleted rather than
    orphaned; SSSD is stopped before its caches are removed. '--check' reports
    what remains, and the same report runs after a removal, so "it is gone"
    is a finding and not an assumption. PAM, local accounts, SSH, sudo, DNS
    and the hostname are deliberately left alone.
  * New scripts/install-desktop.sh, installed as
    /usr/sbin/sssd-smartcard-install-desktop: puts a minimal graphical
    environment on a clean machine so a card login can be tested at the real
    GDM greeter. The install runs detached under systemd, because losing an
    SSH session partway through a ten-minute apt transaction leaves dpkg to
    be untangled by hand; re-running attaches to an install in flight rather
    than starting a second one. The interface carrying remote access is
    marked unmanaged before NetworkManager arrives with the desktop, and the
    address is compared afterwards, since a desktop install that moves the IP
    is what strands a remote machine. Verification covers the desktop and
    Smart Card packages, the default target, GDM, the gdm-smartcard PAM
    alternative and pcscd (socket activation included).
  * New scripts/ad-prepare.sh, installed as /usr/sbin/ad-prepare: sets the DNS
    servers and search domain to the domain controllers and gives the host its
    fully qualified name, before any join is attempted. It detects whether
    netplan, NetworkManager or plain resolv.conf persists DNS on this host,
    refuses to write /etc/resolv.conf when it is a systemd-resolved symlink,
    and applies the resolver through resolvectl so the interface is never
    bounced -- an interface bounce over SSH drops the session running the
    script, usually mid-change. --apply-now opts into the bounce.
    netplan output is validated with 'netplan generate' before the run is
    called a success, so a file that would break networking at the next boot is
    rolled back now rather than discovered later. Verification proves each
    configured server actually answers for the AD zone, that the domain SRV
    records resolve, that the DC accepts connections on 88/389/445/464, that
    the clock is synchronised, and that the host's own FQDN resolves.
  * CRL revocation, with the rules measured rather than assumed. SSSD checks
    the CRL of every CA in the chain, so crl_file must hold one current CRL per
    CA concatenated into a single PEM; one short and verification fails with
    "unable to get certificate CRL" for every card, because crl_file fails
    closed. soft_crl forgives an expired CRL but never a missing one, enabling
    OCSP does not relax the requirement, and partial_chain combined with
    crl_file breaks verification outright -- so partial_chain is now dropped,
    with an explanation, when CRL checking is on.
  * New 'ca crl' commands: show, update and timer. 'update' walks the chain the
    right way round -- a certificate's crlDistributionPoints names its ISSUER's
    CRL, so the issuing CA's CRL is advertised by the card and the root's by the
    issuing CA's certificate -- then refuses to install an incomplete or expired
    set rather than producing a host that rejects every card. The URL used for
    each CA is recorded, so the systemd timer can refresh with no card inserted.
  * doctor reports CRL freshness, chain coverage, the refresh timer and whether
    revocation is enforced at all; it also says plainly when no_ocsp is set with
    no crl_file, which means a revoked certificate still logs in.
  * Add tests that build the CLI parser and parse every documented command. A
    duplicated block had left build_parser() raising "conflicting subparser" --
    the tool could not start, and the whole suite stayed green.
  * Check that an authenticated user can actually get a session: whether
    pam_mkhomedir.so or pam_oddjob_mkhomedir.so is in the session stack, and
    whether the account's home directory exists, is owned by it and is not
    readable by others. A Smart Card login with no home directory succeeds and
    then shows a black screen under GNOME, and nothing in the certificate,
    mapping or PAM checks could see it -- fallback_homedir only names the path.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Sun, 11 Oct 2026 23:55:00 +0200

sssd-smartcard (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Write local_auth_policy = enable:smartcard into the domain section for the
    ad and ldap providers. Without it SSSD reads the card, verifies the
    certificate, maps it to a real user through the certmap rule and then
    declines it -- the PAM responder logs "Local auth policy allowed: smartcard
    [False]" and pam_sss falls through to the password prompt. The option
    defaults to match, which offers only what the backend offers, and the AD
    and LDAP backends offer no Smart Card authentication of their own.
    Version gated at SSSD 2.9.3; ipa is left alone because IPA authenticates
    the card through the backend.
  * Report local_auth_policy in doctor and pam check, so "the card is read and
    then refused" is diagnosable without raising debug_level on a live host.
  * Extend an existing local_auth_policy rather than overwriting it, and refuse
    to touch an existing "only" value, reporting it instead.
  * New profile keys authentication.local_smartcard and
    authentication.local_auth_policy; undocumented policy values are refused
    rather than written into sssd.conf.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Sun, 11 Oct 2026 23:30:00 +0200

sssd-smartcard (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Distribution-aware PAM handling. authselect is Red Hat only; Ubuntu 23.10
    and later use pam-auth-update with the sss-smart-card-optional and
    sss-smart-card-required profiles, and older releases get the exact
    common-auth line instead of a command that does not exist.
  * Group Policy support, keeping the two unrelated meanings apart:
    ad_gpo_access_control in sssd.conf, and Canonical's adsys client with
    optional certificate auto-enrolment through certmonger and cepces.
  * Default ad_gpo_access_control to permissive when managing it, and warn when
    it is left unmanaged: sssd-ad(5) documents the default as enforcing, which
    on a freshly joined host denies login to anyone the domain's GPOs do not
    grant InteractiveLogonRight.
  * Kerberos verification: krb5 status reports the credential cache and its
    TGT, krb5 test checks realm, KDC discovery and reachability, and with
    --pkinit obtains a real ticket with the card into a throwaway cache.
  * Guided setup script with a hard identity-resolution gate, per-stage
    self-verification, backup and rollback, idempotency and a dry run.
  * Rank mapping mechanisms that need per-user directory provisioning below
    ones that work against an untouched directory, and show the penalty.

 -- Szymon Frankiewicz <szymonfrankiewicz@gmail.com>  Sun, 11 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0200

sssd-smartcard (0.1.0) unstable; urgency=low

  * Initial release: Certificate Inspector, mapping recommendation engine,
    certmap renderer with an SSSD version compatibility layer, Mapping Tester
    backed by sssctl cert-eval-rule, CA trust bundle management, PKCS#11
    discovery, trusted-domain diagnostics and safe apply with rollback.

